# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Governance handwritten notes, chapters 25-29. Sources: Sarrthi IAS GS-2 'Civil Society, NGOs, SHG, Pressure Groups' and 'Comptroller and Auditor General of India' handouts (new topics)."""
from dsl import *
from hwlib import P, H, R
OWN = lambda w, t: mn(w, t + ' <i>(own mnemonic)</i>')

CHS = [
dict(toc='Civil society and NGOs', id=25, title='CIVIL SOCIETY &amp; NGOs', part=3, icon='people', prio='H',
 pyq='Mains 2025 (anti-State?) · 2023 · 2021 · 2016 · 2015 (NGOs and environment) · Handout CS pp. 3-9',
 simple='When the state and the market both fail, people organise on their own: civil society, the third sector. NGOs are its main face. Know the features, the four generations, the roles and the problems.',
 nodes=[
 bx('bl', '<b>Governance has two aspects:</b> process/institutions (state, market, NGOs, legal frameworks) and substantive content (how it addresses health, education etc.). In a welfare state like India the <b>state is the principal problem-solving forum</b> and its legitimacy lay in its capacity to address public issues, defining India\'s model until the 1991 reforms. Over time structural inefficiencies showed it could not tackle complex challenges single-handedly; post-1990s reforms shifted to market delivery; three decades on the market\'s exploitative tendencies and limits are evident: so civil society emerged as the <b>third forum</b> (inclusivity, transparency, participatory decisions).'),
 t('<b>Why the State falls short (1st sector)</b>', '<b>overcapitalisation:</b> too much money and reliance on the state yet inadequate benefit to people', '<b>good politics, bad economics:</b> decisions not matched to actual needs', '<b>nanny state:</b> over-dependence on the state', 'too much money to one forum: corruption, embezzlement, stiffening of funds, linkages', '<b>rule-driven bureaucracy:</b> adherence to rules became the goal; whether ultimate goals are met was a limited concern', w=40),
 tb(['Forum', 'Strength', 'Weakness'], ['<b>State (1st sector)</b>', 'welfare, policy, legitimacy till 1991', 'overcapitalised; nanny state; rule-driven; corruption'], ['<b>Market (2nd)</b>', 'alternative delivery of goods and services', 'profit-driven; <b>user-pay</b>; exclusionary; merit goods (health, education) neglected for those who cannot pay'], ['<b>Civil society (3rd)</b>', 'voluntary, collective, autonomous', 'no universal definition']),
 bx('pk', '<b>Civil society = people organising themselves outside state and market</b> (NGOs may get donations from state or market but are created by people and largely outside them): an <b>intermediate space</b>; the "fifth estate" (after legislature, executive, judiciary and media). Voluntary (organised on the principle of voluntarism) · collective interest · autonomous · largely self-supporting · bound by shared values · citizens acting collectively in the public sphere · does <b>not seek formal power</b>. <b>Political parties and media are kept out.</b> <b>No universally accepted definition:</b> a mafia satisfies all the characteristics yet is not civil society. It is <b>not society in general</b> but organised social life: people organising around common beliefs, values, interests (NGOs, interest, advocacy, philanthropic, social-activism groups).'),
 t('<b>Why civil society grew</b>', 'developmental failure of state and market', 'societal learning (skill, will to organise)', 'rise of the governance paradigm', '<b>societal learning:</b> people acquire skill, will and ability to organise outside state and market', 'rejection of the <b>instrumentalist</b> state: state not at the centre of everything or of public-goods delivery; move from state-centric to <b>people-centric</b> development', 'globalisation and information age', '<b>colour revolutions</b> (democratic revolutions of the 1990s in many ex-Soviet republics to topple dictators and restore democracy), Arab Spring', w=36),
 sec('COMPONENTS AND SIGNIFICANCE', 'b'),
 t('<b>Components</b>', '<b>people-serving</b> (general interest: Human Rights Watch, ADR) vs <b>member-serving</b> (pressure groups focus on their own members)', '<b>traditional associations:</b> caste, religion, ethnicity, language; <b>representative:</b> trade, student, peasant unions', 'interest groups: SHGs, pressure groups; philanthropic (Ramakrishna Mission), advocacy (PETA)', 'socio-cultural (sports, recreational clubs); professional, typically member-serving (BCI, IMA); social movements (India Against Corruption)', '<b>NGOs:</b> private, not-for-profit, voluntary, self-governing entities · <b>CBOs:</b> voluntary grassroots entities serving local community need', w=38),
 t('<b>Significance</b>', 'supplements, complements, questions, confronts, substitutes (where government is not active) the state/market', 'advocates social change; pro-active defence of <b>civil liberties</b>: HRW, Amnesty, PUCL', 'strengthens <b>transparency and accountability</b>: ADR, Transparency International; a vigilant civil society ensures government does not deviate from its promise', 'makes de-administered, decentralised development workable', 'evaluates impact of government policies (social audits); <b>training ground</b> for future political leaders', '<b>technical support</b> to government: CPR, IDSA, FICCI', w=40),
 mn('SAFE-SCAN', 'Own anchor for significance: <b>S</b>upplement · <b>A</b>dvocacy · <b>F</b>reedoms defended · <b>E</b>valuate (audit) · <b>S</b>upport (technical) · <b>C</b>ontrol of state power · <b>A</b>ccountability · <b>N</b>ew leaders. <i>(own mnemonic)</i>'),
 sec('ANTI-STATE OR NON-STATE? ' + P('2025'), 'r'),
 two([bx('gr', '<b>Non-State, not anti-State:</b> they operate outside the government machinery, which does not make them anti-State. <b>Criticism is not opposition:</b> in a functioning democracy dissent and watchdog activity strengthen the State by improving accountability. <b>Treating all civil society as suspect weakens democracy:</b> blanket suspicion silences the legitimately voiceless alongside the genuinely problematic actor.')],
     [bx('ye', '<b>The concern is not baseless, only overextended:</b> a small number may misuse funds or work against public interest, so <b>proportionate, transparent regulation</b>, the kind the 2nd ARC envisaged for FCRA, is legitimate. The failure is using that concern to justify blanket restriction.')]),
 bx('bl', '<b>Way forward:</b> treat non-state actors as <b>governance partners, not adversaries</b> (service delivery, advocacy, innovation) · reform FCRA toward proportionality, <b>decentralising administration as 2nd ARC suggested</b>, distinguishing genuine financial misconduct from politically inconvenient advocacy · deepen <b>SHG 2.0</b>, addressing rising NPAs and credit weaknesses so SHGs evolve from a subsistence tool into economic empowerment infrastructure · modernise cooperative governance (PACS computerisation, Model Cooperative Societies Act, Ministry of Cooperation) · <b>formal consultative mechanisms</b> for pressure groups, not street mobilisation and informal lobbying alone · <b>calibrate regulation to risk:</b> target genuine wrongdoing without a chilling effect on legitimate civic work.'),
 sec('SOCIAL CAPITAL AND LEGAL BASE', 'g'),
 two([bx('pk', '<b>Social capital = power of the collective.</b> Two pillars: <b>trust and reciprocity</b>. <b>vs financial capital:</b> both are productive; social capital is <b>everywhere</b> whereas financial capital is confined to financial institutions; social capital is <b>unlimited and infinite</b>, financial is finite.')],
     [tb(['Basis', 'Provision'], ['Art 19(1)(c)', 'right to form associations, unions, cooperatives'], ['Art 43B', 'promote cooperatives'], ['Part IX-B', 'cooperatives'], ['Art 43B', 'State to promote voluntary formation, democratic and effective functioning of cooperatives'], ['State List 32', 'cooperatives; multistate cooperatives governed by central laws'], ['Concurrent List 10, 28', 'Entry 10 trusts; Entry 28 charities, charitable and religious endowments'])]),
 t('<b>NGO features</b>', 'voluntary; initiated and governed by own members, ideally on democratic principles; autonomous from state and market', 'legal entities: trusts, cooperatives, charities, trade unions, not-for-profit companies', 'own written constitution; definite aims and objectives; proper organisational structure', 'ability to mobilise resources locally or outside; well-organised accounting/auditing; continuous evaluation of performance', '<b>government funding:</b> certainty of finance; saves time and energy spent raising funds; expands scope and outreach; regular state-NGO interaction is mutually beneficial', w=40),
 sec('ROLES AND GENERATIONS', 'b'),
 t('<b>Role of NGOs in development</b>', '<b>mediatory:</b> link between citizens and government; brings to government circles people\'s problems that escaped notice', '<b>mobilising:</b> organise Gram Sabha meetings; many SHGs sponsored and developed by NGOs', '<b>consultative:</b> think-tanks give technical inputs; leaders consulted, put on ad-hoc advisory bodies', '<b>educational:</b> vocational training for poor to create self-employment; e.g. SEWA (early 1970s, Ela Bhatt: poor women)', '<b>supplementary/complementary:</b> core developmental areas; <b>last-mile</b> connectivity; address people\'s trust deficit with the state through a sensitive approach', '<b>leadership:</b> principal change agents in certain areas, eyes and ears of government (e.g. issues of sex workers)', '<b>innovation:</b> imaginative interpretation of rules to serve public welfare; experiment until best practice, then replicated across departments', '<b>efficiency:</b> bureaucracy is rule-driven, NGOs are <b>goal-driven</b>; adequate funding raises efficiency of government spending', 'also: highlight loopholes in legislative and institutional framework · enforce standards of accountability and transparency · voice of <b>informed dissent</b> through research and surveys', w=44),
 OWN('MM CESIE', 'Roles: <b>M</b>ediatory · <b>M</b>obilising · <b>C</b>onsultative · <b>E</b>ducational · <b>S</b>upplementary · <b>I</b>nnovation · <b>E</b>fficiency (plus leadership, loopholes, accountability, informed dissent).'),
 tb(['Generation', 'Problem seen', 'Scope'], ['<b>1st</b> relief and welfare', 'defined problems as <b>shortage</b> of something', 'bring relief and welfare to individuals or families'], ['<b>2nd</b> community development (began 1970s)', '<b>local inertia</b>: role became mobilising and organising rather than momentary benefits', 'whole village or larger area, the district'], ['<b>3rd</b> sustainable system development', 'defined problems as <b>institutional and policy constraints</b>; element of <b>activism</b>: regular interaction with government, agitation, newspaper articles, media debates', 'region or nation as a whole'], ['<b>4th</b> (present) hybrid: organisations and movements', 'people not given an adequate <b>mobilising vision</b> for the future; doer (relief, studies, policy constraints) and on-ground activists; alternative mobilising vision for the world', 'national and international']),
 OWN('RCDM', 'Generations in order: <b>R</b>elief · <b>C</b>ommunity · <b>D</b>evelopment-system · <b>M</b>ovement. <i>(sequence kept)</i>'),
 sec('PROBLEMS OF NGOs ' + P('2021') + P('2015'), 'r'),
 hub('NGO PROBLEMS', ['Few resources', 'Uneven presence', 'No coordination', 'Few trained staff', 'State interference', 'Corruption (Jholawalas)', 'Elite, donor control', 'Weak accountability'], 'EIGHT FRICTIONS'),
 t('<b>Problems in detail</b>', '<b>deficiency of resources:</b> pre-1991 closed economy and public-sector dominance limited private donations; NGOs with radical agendas became dependent on government funds, so bureaucratic interference; post-liberalisation corporate donations rose but scarcity persists', '<b>uneven presence:</b> limited networks, absent in large parts of rural India; certain regions attract excess attention (Niyamgiri-Vedanta agitation), others neglected', '<b>lack of coordination:</b> NGOs in similar sectors (health, education, skills) compete for donor recognition; reluctance to pool resources for fear of losing credit: duplication and wastage', '<b>limited outreach/networks</b> reinforce uneven presence; <b>shortage of trained and dedicated workers</b> (technical and managerial expertise) hurts field effectiveness', '<b>state interference:</b> government-funded NGOs face bureaucratic oversight; internal operations become bureaucratised and rule-driven, undermining independence', '<b>corruption:</b> some misuse funds, money laundering, misappropriation; ex-CVC N. Vittal named "Jholawalas" (corrupt NGOs) alongside Netas, Babus, Lalas and Dadas', '<b>elite domination:</b> reliance on corporate donors, rich elites, landlords gives donor-driven agendas, compromising grassroots autonomy', '<b>accountability:</b> government-funded NGOs submit progress reports and audits; those raising funds from public donations or informal channels lack structured accountability, so monitoring is difficult', '<b>no common governance norms:</b> registered under Trusts, Societies, Charitable Institutions, Not-for-Profit Companies: fragmented oversight; RWAs and SHGs not formally registered', '<b>government crackdown:</b> in democracies with weak institutions governments see NGOs as threats for dissent and activism; regulatory scrutiny and funding restrictions on those advocating public causes', w=50),
 tb(['Year', 'PYQ (handout, marks)'], ['2025 (10)', 'Civil Society Organisations are often perceived as anti-State actors rather than non-State actors. Do you agree? Justify.'], ['2023 (15)', 'Contribution of civil society groups for women\'s effective and meaningful participation and representation in state legislatures.'], ['2021 (15)', 'Can civil society and NGOs present an alternative model of public service delivery to benefit the common citizen? Discuss the challenges of this alternative model.'], ['2016 (12.5)', 'In the Indian governance system the role of non-state actors has been only marginal. Critically examine.'], ['2015 (12.5)', 'How can the role of NGOs be strengthened in India for development works relating to protection of the environment? Discuss the major constraints.']),
 ],
 recall=[('Civil society is the', 'third sector, "fifth estate"'), ('Excluded from civil society', 'political parties and media'), ('Two pillars of social capital', 'trust and reciprocity'), ('Four NGO generations', 'relief, community, system, hybrid movement'), ('Art 19(1)(c)', 'form associations, unions, cooperatives')]),

dict(toc='FCRA, State regulation of NGOs, donors', id=26, title='FCRA · REGULATING NGOs · DONOR AGENCIES', part=3, icon='flag', prio='H',
 pyq='Mains 2015 (FCRA) · 2024 (charitable trusts) · 2022 (donor agencies) · Handout CS pp. 9-12',
 simple='The state has tightened control of foreign funding step by step: FCRA 1976 → FCRA 2010 → Amendment Act 2020 → an Amendment Bill 2026 that is still only a Bill. Know which are Acts and which is a Bill.',
 nodes=[
 tl(('1976', '<b>FCRA 1976</b> enacted by Indira Gandhi amid declared suspicion that foreign interests were trying to influence or destabilise India'), ('1982', 'Kudal Commission appointed to investigate activities and foreign funding of certain voluntary organisations; report not placed in public domain'), ('2010', '<b>FCRA, 2010</b> main framework (MHA)'), ('2013', 'Lokpal Act 2013: NGOs receiving substantial foreign contributions fall within Lokpal\'s jurisdiction, strengthening accountability over foreign funds'), ('2014', 'media reports cited a leaked Intelligence Bureau report alleging some foreign-funded NGOs mobilised opposition to projects on nuclear energy, mining, GM crops and industrial development: Kudankulam, uranium mining, POSCO, Vedanta'), ('2020', '<b>FCRA (Amendment) Act, 2020</b>'), ('2022', 'Noel Harper v UoI upholds 2020 provisions'), ('2026', '<b>FCRA Amendment Bill, 2026</b>: still a Bill, with a JPC')),
 bx('ye', '<b>Act vs Bill:</b> FCRA 1976, 2010 and the 2020 Amendment are <b>Acts</b>. The 2026 measure is a <b>Bill referred to a Joint Parliamentary Committee</b>, so it is not law yet (handout wording).'),
 sec('FCRA, 2010', 'b'),
 t('<b>FCRA 2010 (MHA)</b>', 'regulates receipt and utilisation of foreign contribution by individuals, associations, NGOs, trusts and other entities; administered by MHA', '<b>two routes: Registration</b> (fixed period, subject to renewal) and <b>Prior Permission</b> (specific purpose and specific foreign source, generally for organisations without regular FCRA registration)', 'allowed fields: cultural, economic, educational, religious, social', '<b>barred recipients:</b> political parties, election candidates, public servants, organisations of a political nature', 'MHA can suspend or cancel; annual returns and accounts', w=34),
 bx('pk', '<b>Concerns:</b> complex registration and renewal · vague "political nature" · suspension and cancellation · compliance load · admin spend cut by 2020. <b>FCRA Rules:</b> office-bearers declare on religious conversion, sedition, violence; foreign hospitality covered.'),
 tb(['FCRA (Amendment) Act, 2020', 'Change'], ['Public servants', 'cannot receive foreign contribution'], ['Transfer', 'cannot pass it to another person or entity'], ['Administrative expenditure', '<b>50% → 20%</b>'], ['Suspension', 'maximum <b>180 → 360 days</b>'], ['Aadhaar', 'mandatory for key office-bearers'], ['Account', 'first into designated FCRA account, <b>SBI New Delhi Main Branch</b>']),
 OWN('PTAS-AD', '2020 changes: <b>P</b>ublic servants barred · <b>T</b>ransfer barred · <b>A</b>dmin cap 20% · <b>S</b>uspension 360 days · <b>A</b>adhaar · <b>D</b>esignated SBI account.'),
 bx('gr', '<b>Noel Harper v Union of India (2022):</b> SC upheld the major 2020 provisions; receiving foreign contribution is <b>not an absolute right</b>.'),
 sec('FCRA AMENDMENT BILL, 2026 (referred to JPC)', 'r'),
 t('<b>What the Bill proposes</b>', 'certificate <b>ceases</b> if not renewed, no application made, or renewal rejected', '<b>Designated Authority</b> for vesting, supervision, management and disposal of foreign contributions and assets created from them after registration ends', '<b>provisional vesting:</b> assets temporarily in custody, restored if registration revived; <b>permanent vesting:</b> if not restored, assets may vest permanently in the Authority, be used for public purposes, disposal proceeds credited to <b>Consolidated Fund of India</b>', 'prior-permission funds must be received and utilised within the prescribed period; Designated Authority must preserve religious character of places of worship, cannot convert or repurpose them', 'maximum imprisonment for specified offences reduced to <b>one year from five</b>; <b>prior approval of the Central Government</b> before initiating investigation for offences under the Act', w=40),
 two([bx('gr', '<b>Significance:</b> closes a legal gap (framework for assets when certificate expires or is not renewed) · prevents regulatory avoidance (cannot escape scrutiny by letting registration lapse) · protects foreign-funded assets for public purpose · transparency and anti-money-laundering compliance · decriminalisation (reduced imprisonment shows a trust-based regulatory approach).')],
     [bx('pk', '<b>Concerns:</b> disproportionate consequence: even procedural lapses may cost assets · <b>retrospective impact:</b> earlier assets may stay subject to vesting even if the organisation now functions on domestic funds · differential treatment of registered vs prior-permission organisations · due process: no clear hearing or appeal against denial of renewal, more executive discretion · centralisation: prior Central approval for investigation raises institutional-independence concerns.')]),
 bx('bl', '<b>Balance and way forward:</b> regulate to stop laundering, foreign political influence, diversion and security threats, but keep space for health, education, environment, relief. Give a <b>statutory appeal</b> · judicial safeguards before permanent vesting · <b>clean exit</b> from FCRA without forfeiture of legitimate assets · real-time digital disclosure on the FCRA portal · proportionate treatment of technical lapses. <b>Conclusion:</b> stronger executive power needs due process, proportionality and transparency.'),
 sec('CAN CIVIL SOCIETY REPLACE THE STATE? ' + P('2016'), 'g'),
 t('<b>State functions not to outsource</b>', 'defence; public order; external affairs', 'larger societal problems neither civil society nor market can handle: population-control policy, comprehensive national policies, broad problems like communalism and casteism', 'symbolic functions (regularly celebrating Republic Day, Independence Day)', 'framing monetary, fiscal, EXIM, industrial policies', '<b>Verdict:</b> civil society can be <b>one alternative problem-solving forum</b>, but not a complete substitute for the state or the market', w=30),
 sec('DONOR AGENCIES ' + P('2022'), 'b'),
 bx('bl', 'Donor agencies are now significant players in financing India\'s development projects; resources and expertise are valuable, but excessive reliance may undermine <b>community ownership and grassroots participation</b> in setting priorities.'),
 two([t('<b>Against dependence</b>', '<b>reduced community ownership:</b> projects look externally driven, alienate locals (Narmada Dam protests)', '<b>top-down imposition:</b> global narratives over local realities (World Bank ICDS support faced local adaptability challenges)', '<b>bureaucratic complications:</b> donor procedural norms, audits, reporting burden local agencies (MNREGA reporting delays from donor-linked processes)', '<b>weakened accountability:</b> external steering bypasses national oversight, less transparency to locals and democratic institutions', w=26)],
     [t('<b>For involvement</b>', '<b>capacity building:</b> training and technical inputs (UNDP water management, rural India)', '<b>participatory planning:</b> community engagement in design and monitoring (ADB Urban Climate Fund)', '<b>local knowledge:</b> partnerships amplify indigenous experience (Sweden-SEWA boosted women\'s livelihoods)', '<b>global best practice:</b> benchmarks in transparency, gender inclusivity, environmental safeguards', w=26)]),
 tb(['Year', 'PYQ (handout, marks)'], ['2015 (12.5)', 'Examine critically the recent changes in the rules governing foreign funding of NGOs under the FCRA, 1976.'], ['2024 (10)', 'Public charitable trusts have the potential to make India\'s development more inclusive as they relate to certain vital public issues. Comment.'], ['2022 (15)', 'Do you agree that increasing dependence on donor agencies for development reduces the importance of community participation in the development process? Justify.']),
 ],
 recall=[('FCRA 1976 enacted by', 'Indira Gandhi government'), ('Admin expenditure cap (2020)', '50% → 20%'), ('Max suspension (2020)', '180 → 360 days'), ('FCRA 2026 status', 'Bill with a JPC, not yet an Act'), ('Noel Harper (2022)', 'foreign contribution not an absolute right')]),

dict(toc='Self-Help Groups', id=27, title='SELF-HELP GROUPS (SHGs)', part=3, icon='people', prio='H',
 pyq='Mains 2021 · 2020 · 2017 · 2015 · 2014 · 2013 · Handout CS pp. 12-17',
 simple='An SHG is a small informal savings group, mostly rural women, that lends from its own corpus and later links to a bank. Social capital replaces collateral. NABARD started the bank-linkage programme in 1992.',
 nodes=[
 bx('pk', '<b>SHG:</b> principally a <b>rural women\'s movement</b> (not only women-specific; some include men); small, informal associations, <b>not legally registered, not legal entities</b>; largely poor women, preferably of similar socio-economic background; ideal size <b>10-20</b>. Each must save regularly (weekly); a weekly <b>loan corpus</b> is created and lent to members for income-generating ventures, vulnerabilities, productive needs. A <b>savings-led microfinance model</b> to improve earnings and savings and build an economic base to escape poverty, on <b>self-help and collective responsibility</b>: loans carry <b>no security</b> (poor rural women lack the collateral financial institutions demand); repaid because of collective responsibility and <b>peer pressure</b>.'),
 bx('bl', '<b>Bank link:</b> when an SHG matures it is linked to a bank: it deposits its collective corpus, earns interest, and can avail a larger loan. Banks normally follow a <b>1:4 deposit-to-credit</b> ratio (SHG depositing Rs 10,000 a month as corpus is eligible for four times that as loan).'),
 tb(['Type', 'How it works'], ['<b>ROSCA</b> (Rotating Savings and Credit Association)', 'like the urban kitty/committee: each month or week savings pooled; corpus given to one member (name picked by <b>draw of lots</b>); rotates till the last member is selected, then the cycle repeats'], ['<b>ASCA</b> (Accumulated Savings and Credit Association)', 'no rotation; accumulated corpus from regular periodic savings; loans to those who need, on <b>collective decision and joint approval</b>']),
 sec('SHG-BANK LINKAGE (SHG-BLP)', 'b'),
 t('<b>SHG-BLP</b>', '<b>NABARD, 1992</b> started the SHG Bank Linkage Programme; principal self-help promoting agency in the country; mature SHGs get linked to banks', '<b>Microfinance SHGs:</b> set up by financial institutions', '<b>Microfinance Plus SHGs:</b> set up by government agencies and NGOs, with wider development aims', '<b>SHPIs:</b> NABARD, RRBs, SCBs, NGOs, government agencies', '<b>100+ million households</b> covered; <b>84%+</b> SHGs are women\'s: movement largely driven by rural poor women', w=34),
 bx('bl', '<b>Panchsutras (Panchsheel):</b> if SHGs follow these they are of very good quality and banks are willing to lend: <b>regular</b> savings · regular internal lending · regular repayment of loans · regular meetings · regular books of accounts.'),
 OWN('S-I-R-M-B', 'Teacher\'s <b>Panchsutra</b> (five regulars): <b>S</b>avings · <b>I</b>nternal lending · <b>R</b>epayment · <b>M</b>eetings · <b>B</b>ooks of account. <i>(initials are my aid; the five points are the handout\'s)</i>'),
 t('<b>NABARD tools</b>', '<b>Joint Liability Group:</b> the whole group collectively takes responsibility for repaying the loan even if only a small part was lent to one member; introduced by NABARD <b>2005-06</b>; best done by organising women of similar skills into <b>affinity SHGs</b> and developing enterprises for all (each like a shareholder; bank loan goes to the whole enterprise)', '<b>NABFINS</b> (NABARD Financial Services Ltd): to evolve into an ideal MFI, setting governance standards among MFIs, a model for how to approach SHGs especially loaning', '<b>microfinance development programmes:</b> skill development if members lack skills; improve skills or optimise production if they have them', '<b>Livelihood and Enterprise Development:</b> <b>livelihood promotion in clusters</b> of <b>15-30</b> SHGs in contiguous villages (one SHG\'s product can be input for another); <b>handholding</b> 3-6 months (representatives explain the value chain); affinity groups; end-to-end support', '<b>DAY-NRLM:</b> mission aims to bring at least <b>one woman from each identified poor rural household</b> into SHGs; special emphasis on the most vulnerable: manual scavengers, disabled, PVTGs, bonded labour, victims of sexual offences', w=44),
 sec('BENEFITS AND IMPACT ' + P('2021') + P('2020'), 'g'),
 t('<b>Benefits</b>', '<b>financial inclusion:</b> perhaps the best model for extending microfinance: savings, credit, insurance', '<b>physical capital replaced by social capital:</b> poor lack physical collateral; SHGs need none', '<b>capability approach</b> (not dole, due or rights): strengthens capacity and <b>autonomy to make the choices</b> people want in life', '<b>social and economic transformation of women:</b> income meets life-cycle and production needs of family; social status rises; gives economic reforms a <b>human face</b>; declared component of GoI poverty-alleviation strategy; positive effect on demographic and social indicators', '<b>political empowerment:</b> community platform; rural women attend Gram Sabha regularly, take part in panchayat elections', w=44),
 bx('ye', '<b>Dole vs Due:</b> <b>dole</b> = something government gives out of generosity or political will; addresses a shortage of goods at a point in time, never on a sustaining basis. <b>Due</b> = government converts benefits (employment, food security: RTS, FSA) into <b>legally enforceable rights</b>, the <b>Rights-Based Approach</b>. SHG is neither: it strengthens people\'s capacity and so their autonomy of choice, the <b>capability approach</b>.'),
 tb(['Impact', 'Fact'], ['Number', 'over <b>10 million</b> SHGs'], ['Credit', 'collateral-free loans of <b>Rs 87,098 crore</b> to <b>5 million</b> SHGs (by March 2019)'], ['Poverty', 'inculcates habit of thrift; less dependence on moneylenders; economic base to escape poverty; other studies: impact more <b>protectional than promotional</b>'], ['Women', 'stronger and more empowered in mature SHGs; but patriarchy leaves women little decision-making control and many studies see limited effect on economic activity, skills; some SHGs organise community action on child marriage, child labour, liquor ban']),
 sec('ISSUES ' + P('2014') + P('2013'), 'r'),
 t('<b>Issues</b>', '<b>outreach:</b> more successful in South India (undivided Andhra led SHG formation; Kerala got lots of government support; maybe linked to higher education); <b>poorest of the poor left out</b>', '<b>restrictive policies of FIs and banks:</b> banks compel SHGs to work as suits the banks; SHGs wound up, members return to moneylenders (NABARD now gives <b>100% refinancing</b> against SHG loans)', '<b>quality of management and governance:</b> weekly meetings not held, poor attendance; book-keeping weak; membership too large, unmanageable; only leaders benefit more from loans; leadership should change periodically but does not; securing autonomy from government interference', 'inability to take up new productive ventures; unhealthy competition among states; inadequate micro and macro studies; capacity and skill development', '<b>rising NPAs (new concern):</b> outstanding SHG loans Rs 423 crore (2007-08), Rs 452 crore (2018-19); expected to rise due to COVID', '<b>inequality:</b> reduces inequality based on social gaps but may create inequality by education: better-educated women benefit more', w=46),
 bx('gr', '<b>Measures:</b> government as <b>facilitator, not controller</b> (Grameen Bank) · <b>targeted expansion in credit-deficient regions</b> (MP, Rajasthan, North-East, to bridge regional disparity; Kenya\'s rural SHGs, Chamas, cut financial exclusion among women) · <b>financial infrastructure and digital connectivity:</b> integrate with banking networks, ICT, capacity building (AP SERP model: dedicated monitoring cells and ICT tracking performance, credit linkages, transaction histories) · <b>urban and peri-urban SHGs</b> for slum dwellers and urban micro-entrepreneurship (Brazil\'s Bolsa Familia included urban SHGs). Government role example: Bangladesh\'s Grameen Bank showed proactive government support fosters a microfinance ecosystem.'),
 sec('SUCCESS STORIES', 'b'),
 tb(['SHG story', 'Detail'], ['<b>Kudumbashree</b> (Kerala, 1997)', 'very successful Kerala Government SHG programme, started 1997; literally "Prosperity of Family": neighbourhood groups of women → <b>Area Development Society (ADS)</b> at ward level → federated into <b>Community Development Societies (CDS)</b> at gram panchayat or block panchayat; ~3 lakh neighbourhood groups, ~19,500 ADS, ~1,064 CDS, ~46 lakh women'], ['Grihalakshmi SHG', 'West Singhbhum, Jharkhand; national award for best-performing SHG 2018; notable efforts to eradicate child labour locally'], ['Didi ki Rasoi', 'Bihar; community-based canteen with quality, hygienic food; a group of women can affiliate and start their own; began with food for hospitals, now other institutions too'], ['Pangi Hills brand', 'remote tribal area of Himachal; exotic forest products: honey, black cumin, walnuts; members earn up to Rs 2-2.5 lakh a year'], ['COVID', 'masks, sanitisers, protective gear; instrumental in social distancing, masks, quarantine facilities, elderly care; Kudumbashree network dispelled Covid fake news on WhatsApp groups; COVID an opportunity to push digitisation as physical activity stopped']),
 tb(['Year', 'PYQ (handout, marks)'], ['2021 (10)', 'Can the vicious cycle of gender inequality, poverty and malnutrition be broken through microfinancing of women SHGs? Explain with examples.'], ['2020 (15)', 'Micro-finance as an anti-poverty vaccine aimed at asset creation and income security of the rural poor: evaluate the role of SHGs in achieving the twin objectives along with empowering women in rural India.'], ['2017 (15)', 'Emergence of SHGs points to slow but steady withdrawal of the state from developmental activities: examine the role of SHGs and measures taken by GoI to promote them.'], ['2015 (12.5)', 'The SHG Bank Linkage Programme (SBLP), India\'s own innovation, has proved one of the most effective poverty alleviation and women empowerment programmes. Elucidate.']),
 ],
 recall=[('SHG ideal size', '10-20 women'), ('SHG-BLP started', 'NABARD, 1992'), ('Deposit-to-credit ratio', '1:4'), ('JLG introduced', 'NABARD, 2005-06'), ('Kudumbashree started', 'Kerala, 1997')]),

dict(toc='Pressure groups and lobbying', id=28, title='PRESSURE GROUPS &amp; LOBBYING', part=3, icon='bell', prio='H',
 pyq='Mains 2026 · 2025 · 2021 · 2019 · 2017 · 2013 · Handout CS pp. 17-26',
 simple='A pressure group influences government policy without trying to become the government. India has many types: social, professional, institutional, ad hoc and anomie. Learn techniques, problems and a few recent examples.',
 nodes=[
 bx('pk', '<b>Pressure group:</b> hard to define (parties are sometimes included, sometimes excluded); the common definition <b>excludes political parties</b> and covers groups wishing to influence government policies and activities while remaining apart from government. An association of people with common interests who try to achieve them by influencing government; helps <b>social integration, political articulation</b> and acts as a <b>catalyst for change</b>. Definition: "an organised aggregate which seeks to influence the context of governmental decisions without attempting to place its members in formal governmental capacities". <b>Alan R. Ball:</b> firmly part of the political process; reinforce or change the direction of policy but do not wish to become the government. <b>Henry Turner:</b> non-partisan; do not draft party programmes or nominate candidates. <b>Finer:</b> "anonymous empire". <b>R.D. Lambert:</b> "unofficial government". <b>Truman (group theory):</b> a group, on the basis of shared attitudes (the interest), makes claims on other groups for the establishment, maintenance or enhancement of behaviour implied in those attitudes.'),
 t('<b>Genesis</b>', 'in existence ever since governmental machinery could deliver benefits to individuals or groups; took concrete form after the <b>industrial revolution</b> and rise of market economies', 'trusts and monopolies, struggle over <b>tariffs</b>', 'technology and agricultural skills: new problems, desires, needs, new groups', 'state took on <b>welfare functions</b> besides regulation: more power and discretion in state apparatus, so need to pressure it grew', 'dominant sections need State help for economic activity; weak and deprived need it for basic requirements', '<b>freedom of association</b> in democracies: the basic factor', 'welfare state, socialism, positivism: pressure groups as a <b>shield against misuse of government power</b>', 'consequence of inadequacies of political parties in articulating demands of deprived and dominant interests', w=40),
 sec('FEATURES AND INTEREST GROUPS', 'b'),
 two([t('<b>Features</b>', 'organised on social, political, economic, religious, vocational interests; try to dominate political structure and suppress clashing groups', 'represent interests of different parts of society; vary in size and structure', '<b>vitality</b> = ability to influence policy makers, law makers, implementers; influence varies (trade unions, business, professionals strong; a small ad hoc local committee can force quick policy change by mass marches)', 'modern means without renouncing old: financing parties, sponsoring close-associate candidates, getting friendly persons into executive jobs', 'change allegiance quickly; appear and disappear with conditions', 'individuals have lobbying rights but a collective approach exerts stronger pressure and consolidates similar ideas', w=26)],
     [t('<b>Pressure vs interest group</b>', '<b>interest group:</b> formed by people with shared interests, no interest in pressurising government for favourable decisions', '<b>pressure group:</b> organised interest group with regular interface with government, questions or connects with it to win favourable policy', 'a group may start as an interest group and <b>morph</b> into a pressure group as it grows with wider objectives; terms often used interchangeably', 'inevitable in a democracy; their study is intrinsic to understanding dynamics of politics and of policy making', w=26)]),
 bx('ye', '<b>Pressure groups vs political parties:</b> pressure groups do not participate in elections, parties do; pressure groups have a <b>smaller social base</b>, parties a larger one.'),
 sec('TYPES ' + P('2013'), 'g'),
 tb(['Type', 'Example'], ['<b>Social or identity-based</b>', 'two kinds: community service (Singh Sabha movement); mobilisation for political ends (RSS, VHP, All India Sikh Students Federation)'], ['<b>Associational / professional</b>', 'peasants, trade unions, business associations; professionals: Bar Association, Indian Medical Association'], ['<b>Institutional</b> (operate within government)', 'use the government machinery to pressurise government: IAS lobby, IPS lobby'], ['<b>Ad hoc</b>', 'created for a specific purpose or issue; dissolve when resolved: India Against Corruption (Lokpal movement)'], ['<b>Anomie</b>', 'from despair and frustration when regular grievance methods fail: spontaneous outbursts that can turn violent, destroy public property, use extra-legal means: RRB exam protests (Gaya, Patna), MAGA movement, anti-vaxxers (USA)']),
 OWN('SAIAA', 'Five types: <b>S</b>ocial · <b>A</b>ssociational · <b>I</b>nstitutional · <b>A</b>d hoc · <b>A</b>nomie.'),
 sec('RECENT EXAMPLES (as given in the handout; verify dates)', 'b'),
 tb(['Group', 'Role and example'], ['<b>Farmers</b>', 'role: MSP guarantees, fair prices, subsidies, debt relief, land rights via mass mobilisation and negotiation. <b>Samyukt Kisan Morcha 2020-21</b> against three farm laws, <b>repealed in 2021</b>; SKM (Non-Political) and KMM <b>Dilli Chalo 2024</b>: legal MSP guarantee on <b>C2+50%</b> formula, debt waivers, pensions'], ['<b>Youth / Gen Z</b>', 'role: unemployment, paper leaks, corruption, institutional accountability via social media and mass protests. Handout: Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) Jantar Mantar protests 2026 against exam irregularities; CJP activists in October 2026 on alleged electoral-roll irregularities, demanding the Chief Election Commissioner\'s resignation'], ['<b>Trade unions</b>', 'role: wages, job security, conditions, pensions, collective bargaining. Central unions\' nationwide strike 2025 (higher minimum wages, social security, changes to Four Labour Codes); Noida factory workers April 2026: UP raised minimum wage ~21% for unskilled workers in Noida and Ghaziabad'], ['<b>Environment</b>', 'role: biodiversity, pollution control, forest rights, sustainable development. Save Aarey 2019-22 (tree-felling for Mumbai Metro car shed); Hasdeo Arand Bachao 2024 (tribals vs coal mining and forest clearance, Chhattisgarh)'], ['<b>Professional</b>', 'role: professional autonomy, fair pay, ethical standards, workplace safety. IMA nationwide protests 2024 after RG Kar Medical College incident: Central Protection Act, stronger hospital security'], ['<b>Gig workers</b>', 'role: fair wages, algorithmic transparency, social security, protection from arbitrary deactivation. December 2025 strikes (IFAT and others) against unsafe 10-minute delivery targets, low earnings, poor social security']),
 bx('ca', '<span class="tag">In the news · handout (verify)</span>The handout says sustained CJP protests contributed to the resignation of Union Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan in July 2026. Unverified by me.'),
 sec('WESTERN vs INDIAN', 'r'),
 t('<b>Differences</b>', 'Western groups regarded as the "<b>fourth organ</b>" of government; Indian groups not yet able to play a significant role in politics', 'India and Britain: main lobbying targets are the <b>cabinet and civil service</b>, not Parliament; USA: <b>Congress and its committees</b>, rather than the President', 'Indian groups based on caste, religion, region are more powerful than business organisations', 'American groups take interest in foreign policy; Indian groups do not', w=36),
 sec('SIGNIFICANCE', 'g'),
 t('<b>Significance</b>', 'channel of communication between citizens and government', 'broaden political participation: people join because of deep distrust between people and political parties', '<b>political socialisation:</b> process by which people become politically active and acquire political vision, approach, values', 'data, inputs, information for policy (FICCI, ASSOCHAM, CII)', 'prevent a dominant force: countervailing groups demand opposite things', '<b>informal face of politics:</b> mobilise and shape public opinion, question and confront government, agitation and propaganda: <b>keep democracy alive between elections</b>', 'support parties and candidates, give donations', '<b>"legislatures behind legislatures":</b> legislators get cues on what to discuss from groups that raise issues', 'reservoirs of political leadership (Kejriwal from the Lokpal movement)', 'check arbitrary use of state power (farmers vs three farm laws); improve governance (human-rights groups)', 'educate people by campaigns, propaganda, reports, media debates (environmentalists against growth harmful to environment)', '<b>safety valve</b> for grievances, preventing spontaneous outbursts', '<b>consensus building</b> to present a united face to government (FICCI; Samyukt Kisan Morcha)', w=50),
 sec('TECHNIQUES, LOBBYING, BEST PRACTICES', 'b'),
 hub('TECHNIQUES', ['Lobbying (CII, FICCI)', 'PILs (CSE)', 'Media, digital drives', 'Mass protests', 'Report cards, social audit', 'Research, coalitions', 'Satyagraha, fasts', 'RTI exposés (MPLADS)'], 'EIGHT WAYS TO PRESSURE'),
 t('<b>Techniques in detail (with handout examples)</b>', '<b>lobbying and direct negotiation</b> with policymakers, bureaucrats, legislators (CII, FICCI on tax reforms and industrial policy)', '<b>PILs</b> for judicial intervention (CSE for air-pollution control)', '<b>media campaigns and opinion building</b> (RTI activists exposing administrative opacity)', '<b>mass mobilisation:</b> rallies, dharnas, petitions, signature drives (farmers\' protests 2020-21)', '<b>social audits and citizen report cards</b> (PAC report card reduced bribery in service delivery in Bangalore)', '<b>expert inputs and research-based advocacy:</b> white papers, data to committees and ministries (PRS Legislative Research)', '<b>coalition building</b> (NCPRI united civil society for the RTI Act)', '<b>strategic legal interventions:</b> court cases, stay orders, amicus curiae', '<b>symbolic protests:</b> satyagraha, padayatras, hunger strikes (Anna Hazare\'s Lokpal movement)', '<b>digital campaigns:</b> hashtags, viral videos, petitions (#SaveAarey mobilised urban youth)', '<b>RTI-based exposes</b> (MPLADS fund misuse)', '<b>international advocacy and moral pressure:</b> global bodies and indices for reputational pressure', w=50),
 bx('bl', '<b>Best practices:</b> <b>PAC report card</b> (Bangalore: citizen audits cut corruption in municipal services) · <b>NCPRI</b> RTI campaign (coalition success, led to the RTI Act) · <b>#SaveAarey</b> (Mumbai, 2019: digital activism led to policy reversal on urban deforestation) · <b>CEHAT</b> (research-based lobbying shaped national policy on patient rights) · <b>Anna Hazare\'s Jan Lokpal movement</b> (2011: Gandhian methods put anti-corruption on the national agenda).'),
 bx('pk', '<b>Lobbying:</b> so called as big business leaders roamed the lobbies of the US Congress to influence policy. Today: formally connecting with government, regular meetings with officials and ministers, presenting the interests of the section one represents to get a favourable policy outcome; used principally by organised corporate groups, business houses, business associations. <b>Not bad per se; in India neither legal nor illegal.</b> Problems: (a) <b>paid lobbyists:</b> Nira Radia and Vaishnavi Corporate Communications (getting specific leaders placed in specific ministries); Deepak Talwar Associates (accused of bribing civil servants); (b) <b>legislators as lobbyists:</b> H.G. Mudgal disqualified from the provisional Parliament after Independence for lobbying for a private organisation (Bombay Bullion Organisation); (c) <b>bureaucrats, serving or retired:</b> advising the Minister to tweak rules for certain organisations, pushing files to accommodate a private player. <b>Santhanam Committee 1962:</b> proper system of <b>accreditation</b> of lobbyists; no civil servant should interact with a representative of a business organisation or group unless fully accredited.'),
 bx('gr', '<b>Business associations (FICCI, ASSOCHAM, CII) methods:</b> lobbying · political donations via electoral bonds · critical assessment of government economic policies/legislation · commissioning studies · workshops and conferences · technical inputs · appointment to advisory/ad hoc panels · consensus building · interaction with analogous institutions abroad, like WTO.'),
 sec('PROBLEMS AND EFFECTIVENESS', 'r'),
 t('<b>Problems</b>', '<b>disproportionate influence</b> of some: caste and community groups, trade and peasant unions, business associations like FICCI, CII after 1991', '<b>subversion of democratic institutions:</b> legislators and parties heavily influenced lose independent thinking', '<b>unorganised interests neglected</b> (e.g. educational development)', '<b>extra-legal methods:</b> bandhs, hartals, rail roko, chakka jam, destruction of public property', '<b>divisive:</b> prevent emergence of a true national identity', '<b>block progressive initiatives:</b> some Khap Panchayats sanction honour killings in inter-caste marriages', 'many lack transparency in internal functioning (e.g. RSS)', w=40),
 bx('ye', '<b>Khap Panchayats:</b> SC <b>2011</b>: a Khap advocating or sanctioning honour killing should be banned altogether under the Unlawful Activities (Prevention) Act; an officer who does not check its illegal decisions would also be penalised. As victims are generally SC/ST, the accused can also be prosecuted under the <b>SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989</b>. Some states like Rajasthan proposed laws against honour killing, but regular IPC provisions (e.g. Sec 302) suffice if rationally enforced. Khaps thrive on weak local bodies, judicial inadequacies, political patronage. <b>But</b> some Khaps have done consensus building and socially oriented community service.'),
 two([bx('bl', '<b>Effectiveness depends on:</b> <b>sectional</b> (RSS, Khap Panchayats, caste associations: more effective in India) vs <b>promotional</b> (human rights organisations, PETA) · <b>funding</b> (lobbying needs a lot of money) · <b>political branding</b> (linked to certain parties, so effectiveness depends on that party\'s role in government) · <b>membership</b> (quantity and quality) · <b>government reliance on them</b> (Make in India needs business associations).')],
     [bx('gr', '<b>Way forward:</b> <b>legal recognition and regulatory framework:</b> absence of formal status limits legitimacy and accountability; registration and regulation akin to US and UK lobbying laws · <b>code of conduct and financial transparency:</b> mandatory ethics, disclose funding sources and lobbying (vital in telecom, pharma where opaque lobbying undermines trust) · <b>consultative role</b> in legislative committees and policy dialogues (e.g. farmers\' associations in agricultural reforms and MSP) · <b>digital platforms like MyGov</b> to gather feedback systematically.')]),
 tb(['Year', 'PYQ (handout, marks)'], ['2026 (15)', 'To what extent do pressure groups, social movements and corporate lobbies deepen pluralistic democracy in India by representing excluded interests? Analyse whether the growing convergence of corporate wealth and political power threatens the autonomy of formal democratic institutions.'], ['2025 (15)', 'What are environmental pressure groups? Discuss their role in raising awareness, influencing policies and advocating environmental protection in India.'], ['2021 (10)', 'Pressure groups play a vital role in influencing public policy making in India. Explain how the business associations contribute to public policies.'], ['2019 (10)', 'Methods used by farmers\' organisations to influence policymakers in India and how effective are they?'], ['2017 (10)', 'How do pressure groups influence the Indian political process? Do you agree that informal pressure groups have emerged as more powerful than formal ones in recent years?'], ['2013 (10)', 'Pressure group politics is sometimes seen as the informal face of politics. Assess the structure and functioning of pressure groups in India.']),
 ],
 recall=[('Five types', 'social, associational, institutional, ad hoc, anomie'), ('Groups vs parties', 'groups do not contest elections'), ('Lobbying in India', 'neither legal nor illegal'), ('Santhanam 1962 on lobbyists', 'accredit them'), ('Khap ruling', 'SC 2011: ban honour-killing sanction')]),

dict(toc='Comptroller and Auditor General of India', id=29, title='COMPTROLLER &amp; AUDITOR GENERAL (CAG)', part=3, icon='scale', prio='H',
 pyq='Mains 2018 (appointment, terms, powers) · 2016 (Art 149; policy audit) · Handout CAG pp. 2-7',
 simple='The CAG audits the spending of Union, States and bodies funded by the government, and reports to Parliament. India\'s CAG audits after the money is spent; the UK CAG also controls release of funds.',
 nodes=[
 bx('pk', '<b>Ambedkar</b> in the Constituent Assembly: "the most important office under the Constitution"; one of the <b>4 pillars</b> with SC, UPSC and Election Commission. <b>Art 148</b> creates the office. Principal responsibility: audit accounts of State and Central governments spending taxpayers\' money; so significant for financial accountability that it must have independence like the judiciary. <b>Status:</b> service conditions broadly equivalent to a <b>Supreme Court judge</b>, including salary and removal safeguards.'),
 tb(['Point', 'Fact'], ['Tenure', '<b>6 years or until age 65</b>, whichever is earlier, under the <b>CAG (Duties, Powers and Conditions of Service) Act, 1971</b>; changes require amending the Act, not the Constitution'], ['Post-retirement restriction', '<b>Art 148(4):</b> CAG ineligible for further government office under the Union or any State'], ['Duties', 'prescribed under <b>Art 149</b>; laid down in the <b>CAG (Duties, Powers and Conditions of Service) Act, 1971</b>, enacted by Parliament'], ['Accounts form', 'advises President (<b>Art 150</b>)'], ['Net proceeds', 'certifies shared Union-State proceeds (<b>Art 279</b>)']),
 sec('FUNCTIONS', 'b'),
 t('<b>Audit and report on</b>', '<b>all expenditure</b> from Consolidated Fund, Public Account, Contingency Fund of India / States / UTs with legislative assembly', '<b>trading, manufacturing accounts and profit and loss statements</b> kept by any department of Centre, States, UTs with legislature (e.g. Railways, Posts)', '<b>receipts</b> of Union and States: rules designed to secure an effective check on assessment and collection of revenue', '<b>stocks and stores</b> and government inventory: ensure no pilferage', 'receipts and expenditure of bodies <b>substantially financed</b> by government; and of bodies not substantially financed when <b>requested by the President or Governor</b>', '<b>Public Sector Undertakings</b>', '<b>advises the President</b> on the form of accounts of Union and States (<b>Art 150</b>); certifies net proceeds shared by Union with States (<b>Art 279</b>)', '<b>assists the Finance Commission</b> in a fair understanding of states\' actual financial position', 'audits accounts of <b>Autonomous District Councils</b> under Schedule VI', w=50),
 OWN('CARSE', 'Audit-on: <b>C</b>onsolidated and other funds · <b>A</b>ccounts (trading, P&amp;L) · <b>R</b>eceipts · <b>S</b>tores · <b>E</b>ntities financed (bodies, PSUs).'),
 sec('PSU AUDIT AND ACCOUNTS', 'g'),
 tb(['PSU type', 'CAG audit'], ['<b>Statutory corporations</b> (special law, governed by own statute, generally government-owned; LIC, FCI, RBI)', 'CAG may conduct <b>full, partial or no audit</b>, depending on the law'], ['<b>Government companies</b> (registered under Companies Act / joint stock; at least 51% government ownership)', 'audited by <b>private auditors appointed in consultation with CAG</b>; CAG can also do a <b>supplementary audit</b> over and above it']),
 bx('bl', '<b>PSUs</b> = government-owned enterprises for commercial and economic activity outside the regular departmental structure; some set up as State monopolies in strategic sectors. <b>Performance audit:</b> both categories may face comprehensive performance audit through the <b>Audit Board (introduced 1968)</b>, where applicable.'),
 bx('bl', '<b>Separation of accounting and auditing:</b> accounts are prepared by the department or undertaking; audit is by an external authority for independence and no conflict of interest. <b>Departmentalisation of Accounts (1976):</b> before 1976 CAG did both compilation of accounts and audit; the scheme transferred preparation and compilation of Union accounts to ministries and departments, leading to the <b>Indian Civil Accounts Service (ICAS)</b> and the <b>Controller General of Accounts (CGA)</b> under the Finance Ministry. <b>Now:</b> CAG primarily audits Union accounts, continuing both accounting and auditing for most States, subject to arrangements. <b>Significance:</b> clearer separation, stronger financial accountability and independent oversight.'),
 sec('ISSUES IN WORKING ' + P('2018'), 'r'),
 t('<b>Mode of appointment</b>', '<b>no role of a broad-based committee</b> as in NHRC, CVC, Information Commissioners; entirely at the <b>discretion of the central government</b>', 'UK: CAG heading the National Audit Office appointed by <b>House of Commons resolution</b> after agreement between PM and chair of PAC (generally an opposition member)', 'USA: Comptroller General heading GAO appointed by the President, <b>ratified by the Senate</b>', 'Australia: ratification and confirmation by the <b>whole Public Accounts Committee</b>', 'India is a member of ASOSAI and INTOSAI and should abide by their mission statement and charter so that complete independence is guaranteed; experts recommend a <b>broad-based committee of PM, LoP in Lok Sabha and CJI</b> for appointments like CAG, UPSC, Election Commission', w=44),
 t('<b>Who should be appointed</b>', 'experts: <b>career auditors and accountants of the Indian Audit and Accounts Service (IAAS)</b>', 'till date among <b>14</b> CAGs only the first three came from IAAS; of the remaining 11, <b>10 were retired IAS</b> officers', '<b>adverse effect on morale and motivation of IAAS officers</b>', '<b>conflict of interest:</b> an IAS officer who served a department is asked to audit the same department as CAG', 'post-retirement incentive for IAS officers may lead to <b>politicisation of civil services</b>', 'so: <b>encadre</b> the post, earmarked only for IAAS members', '<b>Narhari Rao</b> first CAG; <b>K. Sanjay Murthy</b> present (as per handout, verify)', w=40),
 bx('ye', '<b>Single vs multi-member body:</b> experts debate making CAG multi-member like UPSC and Election Commission. But the real problem is <b>understaffed IAAS</b>, not single-member form: of about <b>250,000</b> auditable entities CAG effectively audits only about <b>60,000</b> a year, so more IAAS officers, ground staff and junior officers are needed. In the USA and UK too the CAG is a single-member body, with a heavier caseload than India\'s; no study shows a multi-member institution performs better.'),
 sec('TYPES OF AUDIT', 'b'),
 tb(['Audit', 'Question it asks'], ['<b>Compliance</b> (legality / statutory / transactions audit)', 'was money spent according to the financial law of the country? legal availability of money spent; was the mandated activity authorised by Parliament?'], ['<b>Financial</b>', 'are proper books of accounts maintained; are recorded transactions a faithful representation of what actually took place?'], ['<b>Performance</b> (includes efficiency audit)', 'e.g. number of schools set up under an amount, and their standards and facilities: efficiency of money spent. CAG does only performance audit/<b>reporting, not performance evaluation</b>; reasons for inefficiency are outside CAG\'s domain'], ['<b>Propriety</b> (from "proper")', 'questions the <b>wisdom and faithfulness</b> of government spending; e.g. 2G spectrum on first-come-first-served, audited by then CAG Vinod Rai: Rs 1.76 lakh crore revenue loss. <b>Need:</b> it is taxpayers\' money, not officials\' own, so officials cannot be expected to show the caution they would with their own; major frauds and scams come to light only through propriety audit']),
 OWN('CFPP', 'Four audits: <b>C</b>ompliance · <b>F</b>inancial · <b>P</b>erformance · <b>P</b>ropriety. The 1971 Act does not clearly state the types of audit; <b>Sec 23</b> authorises CAG to define the scope of audit.'),
 two([bx('pk', '<b>Against propriety audit:</b> <b>benefit of hindsight:</b> a decision may look unwise without the context in which it was made · <b>auditors know little of administration:</b> seasoned auditors lack experience of decisions under administrative circumstances · <b>red tapism:</b> fear of CAG, CVC, CBI makes officials keep files undecided · <b>slows development</b>. <b>Paul Appleby</b> (1950s, American public-administration expert): CAG oversight created fear among civil servants and delayed decisions; he even recommended <b>abolishing</b> the office.')],
     [bx('gr', '<b>For:</b> CAG\'s role is more than tallying accounts, "which any B.Com. graduate can do" · supreme audit institutions worldwide do propriety audit · CAG should not be nit-picking, but if a <b>prima facie case of fraud, impropriety, irregularity</b> comes up, exposing it is not just his right but his <b>duty</b> · purpose is not fault-finding but <b>better financial governance</b> · instead of questioning it, <b>streamline internal audits</b> and respond proactively to audit queries · best potential to expose irregularities, embezzlement, bribery, fraud.')]),
 bx('bl', '<b>2G case:</b> 2008 licences allocated on First-Come-First-Served instead of auctions; CAG estimated a presumptive loss of up to <b>Rs 1.76 lakh crore</b>; government defended low spectrum prices as promoting affordable telecom; <b>SC 2012</b> quashed <b>122</b> licences and directed fresh allocation by auction; the auction raised about <b>Rs 9,400 crore</b>, below expectations, partly due to changing market conditions and limited bidders. <b>CAG\'s jurisdiction:</b> normally examines policy <i>implementation</i>, but may question policy decisions where they involve financial irregularities, wasteful expenditure or loss to the exchequer ' + P('2016')),
 sec('COMPTROLLER vs AUDITOR; PPPs; LOCAL BODIES', 'g'),
 bx('bl', '<b>Comptroller function:</b> before public money is released, verify that the expenditure has been <b>authorised by Parliament</b> and approve release of funds. <b>Key difference:</b> UK CAG exercises <b>pre-expenditure</b> control; Indian CAG audits <b>after</b> the money has been spent.'),
 tb(['', 'UK', 'India'], ['<b>Comptroller</b> (pre-expenditure control over release of funds)', 'performed', '<b>not</b> performed'], ['<b>Auditor</b> (post-expenditure)', 'yes', 'yes (primary role)']),
 t('<b>Should CAG audit PPPs?</b>', '<b>PPP:</b> government-private collaboration for infrastructure (highways, airports, ports); government gives approvals, land, support as agreed; private gives investment and expertise, develops and operates', '<b>BOT:</b> private builds and operates for a concession period, recovers investment through tolls or user fees, then transfers to government', '<b>VGF:</b> one-time or deferred government grant for economically justified but financially unviable PPP projects; <b>2020 VGF Scheme</b> extended to social infrastructure (health, education, water supply, waste management)', 'question: PPPs may be largely privately financed, so what is CAG\'s jurisdiction? <b>Public interest:</b> public assets, natural resources, concessions, revenue sharing; irregularities in concession period, toll collection, revenue sharing harm public interest; so PPPs <b>may fall in CAG purview</b>, depending on applicable law and contract, even with private financing', '<b>Telecom revenue sharing:</b> operators pay licence fees as a percentage of <b>Adjusted Gross Revenue (AGR)</b>; underreporting or inadmissible deductions cut the government\'s share; <b>AUSPI v Union of India (2014):</b> SC upheld CAG\'s authority to examine private telecom operators\' accounts to verify revenue-sharing payments', w=50),
 bx('ye', '<b>Local bodies (ULBs, PRIs):</b> in some states CAG provides <b>broad supervision and guidelines</b> for local-body accounts and audit; it does <b>not conduct audits as such</b>; the broad overview is accepted by state governments principally to get the <b>conditional grants recommended by the Finance Commission</b>.'),
 sec('WHY CAG IS WEAK: THE FAMOUS QUOTE', 'r'),
 tb(['"CAG is a ..."', 'Meaning'], ['<b>prosecutor</b> with a law that hobbles his functioning', 'law does not clearly indicate the audits he is to perform; does not give adequate powers to obtain documents from government; audit queries are not answered properly, yet law gives no power to act against departmental officials who refuse; so the law itself makes him shaky'], ['<b>judge</b> without the power to sentence', 'can clearly indicate a case of financial fraud but cannot punish; can only make allegations in the report to Parliament; action stays at the <b>government\'s discretion</b>; can establish guilt but not pass punishment'], ['<b>litigant</b> without the right to appeal', 'after his report goes to Parliament and then the PAC, he has no power to question or appeal on action taken; if Parliament and government pay no heed to his report he has no power to challenge it']),
 bx('gr', '<b>Effectiveness of an audit depends on (1) system for evaluating CAG reports:</b> by Parliament and PAC: adequate discussion time, members present. In the USA and UK the CAG is <b>part of the legislative branch</b>; in India independent of both legislature and executive; a long-pending reform is to make CAG part of the legislature so Parliament debates reports more seriously, holding government accountable and improving financial governance.'),
 bx('gr', '<b>(2) Quality of audit reports:</b> under <b>INTOSAI</b> guidelines senior auditors from other countries review India\'s audit process and make recommendations (and Indian auditors go abroad): the <b>peer review mechanism</b>. First peer review of India\'s CAG in <b>2012</b> found around <b>50%</b> of CAG\'s irregularity allegations were <b>not backed by concrete evidence</b>.'),
 tb(['Year', 'PYQ (handout, marks)'], ['2018 (10)', 'The CAG has a very vital role to play. Explain how this is reflected in the method and terms of his appointment as well as the range of powers he can exercise.'], ['2016 (12.5)', 'Exercise of CAG\'s powers in relation to accounts of the Union and States is derived from Art 149. Discuss whether audit of the Government\'s policy implementation could amount to overstepping CAG\'s jurisdiction.']),
 ],
 recall=[('CAG article; tenure', 'Art 148; 6 years or 65'), ('Governing Act', 'CAG (DPC) Act, 1971'), ('Comptroller function', 'UK yes; India no'), ('CAGs from IAAS', 'first 3 of 14'), ('Peer review 2012 finding', '~50% allegations lacked evidence')]),
]
